  urldate = {2023-09-21},
  abstract = {Italy hosts a plethora of stream typologies with very high biodiversity and a number of endemic species unique in Europe, due to its geographical (from the Alps to the Mediterranean) and consequent climatic conditions. Rivers and streams in fact range from Alpine glacial-fed streams, to large lowland rivers, Apennine rivers with distinct features if facing the Adriatic or the Tyrrhenian sea, and ephemeral streams in the south and main island (Sardinia and Sicily). The biogeographic setting, land-use patterns, climate, geomorphology, hydrology, biochemistry, plant and animal biodiversity, management, and conservation of the main Italian rivers are presented. Other rivers are considered because of particular interest due to their ecological integrity or as representative of the many river typologies that characterize Italian running waters (Brenta, Sangro, Amendolea, Flumendosa and Alcantara).},
